Everything above the rule is the PolyForm Shield License 1.0.0, verbatim and
unmodified; do not edit it. The Required Notice line is the one thing this
license expects the licensor to supply, and the Notices section makes it
travel with every copy anyone receives. Left as a placeholder, the licensor
is unnamed — and an unnamed licensor is a Noncompete term with nobody
standing behind it.

Format follows the example in the Notices section above, e.g.
  Required Notice: Copyright Yoyodyne, Inc. (http://example.com)

Why Shield and not MIT/Apache (the locked decision, recorded so the next
reader does not relitigate it): Shield lets anyone read, run, fork and modify
FullShot for any purpose EXCEPT shipping a product that competes with it. It
is source-available, not OSI open source, and GitHub will label it
"non-standard" or show no license badge. That is expected.

It creates no conflict with any of the three stores. Chrome Web Store,
Microsoft Edge Add-ons and addons.mozilla.org all distribute proprietary and
source-available extensions; none require an OSI license. AMO asks for
reviewable source only when a submission is minified or obfuscated, and
FullShot ships unminified. See publish/SUBMISSION-PACKET.md.
